retro:proof The bead's REJECTED alternative — ask the AST node for the heading's written form — was re-measured at the point of use, because the code comment I shipped restates it and an inherited claim reads exactly like a measured one.
evidence: goldmark v1.8.5 in this container, /root/go/pkg/mod/github.com/yuin/goldmark@v1.8.5.
(1) sed -n '200,230p' ast/block.go ->
    // A Heading struct represents headings like SetextHeading and ATXHeading.
    type Heading struct {
        BaseBlock
        // Level returns a level of this heading.
        Level int
    }
    ... var KindHeading = NewNodeKind("Heading")  // one kind for both forms
(2) grep -rn "NewHeading(" parser/ ast/ -> parser/setext_headings.go:70, parser/atx_heading.go:95, parser/atx_heading.go:103 all call ast.NewHeading(level); no other constructor and no form argument.
(3) grep -n "SetAttribute|Attribute" parser/setext_headings.go parser/atx_heading.go -> the only SetAttribute calls are attrNameID and parseLastLineAttributes, i.e. author-written {#id} attributes gated on the opt-in WithHeadingAttribute option; neither parser sets anything that names the written form.
Conclusion: the public AST genuinely does not carry the distinction on the pinned version, so comparing the line-start and text-start offsets is not a workaround for a simpler API that exists. That is why the comment on prefixed() says so — without it the next reader "simplifies" it back to the forward scan that caused this bug.

description:
Implements step 6 in `docs/plans/sprint-skill-design.md`.

`sprint-review` invokes the reviewer declared in `sf.toml` and routes what comes back. What is ours is not the reviewing — it is everything that happens to the findings:

- scoping the review to **this run's** diff, the manifest's `base_sha` to sprint head;
- filing each finding as a bead, `--discovered-from` its origin, parented into the run epic;
- classifying in-scope vs out-of-scope **against the authorization gate** — unauthorized scope growth means file the beads and do not admit them;
- feeding the coordinator's loop: the pass counter, and the decision to re-enter at 3+4.

**Review files; it does not groom.** That is why the loop re-enters at 3+4 rather than dispatching directly: a review finding becomes a bead like any other and earns its readiness the same way, or bounces the same way.

**Undeclared reviewer means skip step 6 and say so in the report.** A silently skipped review reads exactly like a clean one.

The loop itself stays in the coordinator. This skill owns finding things; the coordinator owns the counter and the budget, because those are routing.

comments.text:
retro:stale The bead's site-4 analysis is incomplete: an earlier guard already blocked the never-ran path it describes.

The bead says of closePhases: "The guard above it is `if !ok || closableRec.Failed()`, and `func (r Record) Failed` ... opens with `if r.Status != StepRan { return false }` — so a record that never ran passes the guard and yields `[][]string{nil}`."

evidence: it passes THAT guard and is then stopped by the next one. Read from cli/swarm/cli/phase.go, in order:

    closableRec, ok := closableReport.Record("closable")
    if !ok || closableRec.Failed() { ... RefuseUnrepairable ... }
    closableAnswer, err := tracker.ClosableFrom(closableRec.Value)
    if err != nil { return Result{}, RefuseUnrepairable("%s", err) }
    closableInv := argvOf(closableRec)      // <- the bead's site

and cli/swarm/tracker/tracker.go:

    func ClosableFrom(value any) (Closable, error) {
        if value == nil {
            return Closable{}, fmt.Errorf("tracker: the step produced no value")
        }

A record that never ran carries a nil Value (cli/swarm/plan/run.go sets Value only on the StepRan path), so ClosableFrom refuses before closableInv is ever built. The site-4 `[][]string{nil}` was therefore not reachable even in principle.

The repair still landed as designed — invocationOf in place of argvOf — because the reachable half of site 4 is real: closeEligible's `close-N` records ARE refused in a dry run, which is where the citation was being dropped. That half is now covered by TestPhaseCloseCitesEveryFactWhenNoCloseRan, and it fails against the pre-fix code for the stated reason (negative control run, output below).

retro:decision The three refusal sites were consolidated behind one helper rather than repeated inline, so the guard has a seam a test can reach.

The bead's Design pinned the remedy ("Guard the empty case at the call site and return RefuseUnrepairable naming what could not be cited") but not its shape. Written inline it is three near-identical guards with no callable seam — and since the never-ran path at all three is unreachable end to end (see the unverifiable finding), an inline guard would have had NO test at all.

evidence: cli/swarm/cli/ready.go now carries

    func invocationsRequired(what string, records []plan.Record) ([][]string, error) {
        inv := invocationsOf(records)
        if len(inv) == 0 {
            return nil, RefuseUnrepairable(
                "%s names no invocation a reader could re-run, so its answer is not reported", what)
        }
        return inv, nil
    }

called from acceptDispatch (accept.go), recheckDispatchBeads (accept_settle.go) and reportAgentQueue (ready.go), each passing its own subject string. The guard is still at the call site — each site decides whether to refuse and what to name — and the refusal is now reachable by TestInvocationsRequiredRefusesAReadNoRecordCites.

Rejected: three inline `if len(inv) == 0 { return ..., RefuseUnrepairable(...) }` blocks. It would have cost an untestable guard at each of the three sites and a third copy of the same sentence to keep in agreement.

design:
Anchors: `cli/swarm/cli`, `cli/cmd`-style command registration under
`cli/swarm`.

Relied upon, not reimplemented: `Swarm Command Surface` (the five contract
flags, the run-scope refusal, both renderings, the exit-status mapping);
`Plan Runner` (a step does not run when the step before it failed);
`Process Runner`; [[contract-swarm-json]]'s fact kinds;
[[swarm-output-standards]].

Change Guidance: WHEN a command is added, the plan it builds SHALL be
asserted by a unit test that runs no subprocess. Any new tracker subcommand
goes into TRK with its own decoder — never reach the Process Runner here.

description:
Deferred out of ADR-020 by the repository owner on PR #22, so that decision stays scoped to the FRD lifecycle.

ADR-020 originally asserted "an FRD is a living document; an ADR is an immutable record". The second half is wrong as stated, and the owner named two counter-cases where an ADR is amended without its decision being reversed, and so without calling for a successor:

  * recording an additional positive or negative consequence discovered after the decision was made, where the decision itself still holds;
  * recording an additional option that was considered, where none of the new options changes which option was selected.

ADR-020 now records both under Non-Goals and explicitly does NOT imply ADR immutability.

What this bead must decide:
  * which kinds of change are amendments in place, and which require a successor;
  * where the line sits between the two — the hard part, since "adds a consequence" and "changes the decision" are not always distinguishable from the diff;
  * whether an amended ADR records the amendment (a dated note, a changelog section) or is silently edited, given that the ADR is a record of a decision at a point in time;
  * what this means for `superseded_by`, which the profile registers as the successor property, and for the `retired` lifecycle status.

Not urgent: the framework has functioned without it. It becomes urgent the first time someone needs to add a consequence to an active ADR and has no rule.
